# Stargazing: lying back on a hilltop or at the end of the Hotel Ancora's pier at night, looking up
# while the sky turns. The game's own sky (src/sky.jo, src/celestial.jo: the moon, the planets)
# is overhead, the stars among it (src/night_sky.jo); this names the brighter stars at their places
# on the same celestial sphere: their names (and the constellations') in period Greek and in
# English, shown faintly as one looks at them, and only as far as the sky shows the star itself.
#
# The sphere: the game's sky is a fixed summer, its celestial pole 20 degrees up toward +z (north),
# the sun at right ascension 6h. A star at right ascension ra (hours) and declination dec (degrees)
# stands at hour angle LST - ra, LST running with the clock. The names are transliterated: the
# game's fonts have no Greek letters.

use 'celestial.jo'

struct SkyName:
    greek: str
    english: str
    ra: f64                         # hours
    dec: f64                        # degrees
    mag: f64                        # how bright (lower is brighter); constellations 9
    group = false                   # a constellation (its name over its middle), not a star

